body:
You were right that the `proxy-direct` body was dead code — nothing in the test suite asserts on it. The reason is architectural: undici sends `CONNECT host:port` to the proxy (handled by `proxyServer.on("connect", ...)` further down), so the `createServer` request callback is never invoked by any of our actual proxy-routing tests.

Your suggested fix (return the same body as origin + add an `X-E2E-Proxy` header) doesn't quite fit our setup because the proxy uses CONNECT tunneling — it just pipes bytes between client and origin and never sees response headers, so we couldn't add one even if we wanted to.

Instead, in aeaa78b31 I:

1. Replaced the unreachable `proxy-direct` JSON with a clean `502 Bad Gateway` and a comment explaining the proxy is CONNECT-only.
2. Added a TDD-style test that explicitly asserts the 502 behavior, so future edits can't silently reintroduce a code path that no test exercises:

```ts
describe("test proxy server contract", () => {
  it("responds 502 to direct (non-CONNECT) HTTP requests", async () => {
    const response = await fetch(`${proxyUrl}/anything`)
    expect(response.status).toBe(502)
  })
})
```

This makes the test fixture's contract explicit and verifies it. Thanks for the catch.
